 ongoing social scientific debates about the sources and consequences of inequality, as well as the types of public policy that might appropriately be pursued to reduce (or increase) inequality. These topics will be addressed in related units, some of which include guest lectures by faculty from other universities (funded by the Center for the Study of Inequality). Each unit culminates with a highly spirited class discussion and debate.", "catalogBreadth": "", "catalogDistr": "", "catalogEnrollmentPriority": null, "catalogLang": "", "catalogForbiddenOverlaps": "", "catalogAttribute": "(CU-SBY)", "catalogWhenOffered": "Fall.", "catalogComments": "", "catalogPrereqCoreq": "", "catalogPrereq": null, "catalogCoreq": null, "catalogFee": "", "catalogSatisfiesReq": "", "catalogPermission": "", "catalogCourseSubfield": "", "catalogOutcomes": null, "acadCareer": "UG", "acadGroup": "IL", "lastTermsOffered": null, "exploreCriteriaIds": null}, {"strm": 2741, "crseId": 367804, "crseOfferNbr": 1, "subject": "ILROB", "catalogNbr": "3270", "titleShort": "Social Capital & Organizations", "titleLong": "Social Capital and Organizations", "crseAttrs": [], "crseAttrValueGroups": [], "enrollGroups": [{"classSections": [{"ssrComponent": "LEC", "ssrComponentLong": "Lecture", "section": "001", "classNbr": 16552, "meetings": [{"classMtgNbr": 1, "timeStart": "08:40AM", "timeEnd": "09:55AM", "startDt": "08/29/2019", "endDt": "12/10/2019", "instructors": [{"instrAssignSeq": 1, "netid": "bar93", "firstName": "Ben", "middleName": "A", "lastName": "Rissing"}], "pattern": "TR", "facilityDescr": "Ives Hall 111", "bldgDescr": "Ives Hall", "facilityDescrshort": "IVS111", "meetingTopicDescription": ""}], "notes": [], "campus": "MAIN", "campusDescr": "Ithaca", "location": "ITH", "locationDescr": "Ithaca, NY (Main Campus)", "startDt": "08/29/2019", "endDt": "12/10/2019", "addConsent": "N", "addConsentDescr": "No Special Consent Required", "isComponentGraded": true, "crseAttrs": [], "instructionMode": "P", "instrModeDescrshort": "In Person", "instrModeDescr": "In Person", "topicDescription": "", "openStatus": "O", "exploreCriteriaIds": null, "materials": []}], "unitsMinimum": 4, "unitsMaximum": 4, "componentsOptional": [], "componentsRequired": ["LEC"], "gradingBasis": "GRI", "gradingBasisShort": "GradeNoAud", "gradingBasisLong": "Letter grades only (no audit)", "simpleCombinations": [], "sessionCode": "1", "sessionBeginDt": "08/29/2019", "sessionEndDt": "12/10/2019", "sessionLong": "Regular Academic Session", "exploreCriteriaIds": null}], "description": "How do networks of informal relationships (also termed \"social capital\") affect work outcomes? This course will examine work and employment from a social network perspective, thus investigating how actors are embedded in broader systems of social relations and interactions. The class will also investigate how network structures and exchanges may shape access to resources and provide public signals, thus affecting key organizational outcomes. Topics include how individuals' social connections may shape labor market outcomes, performance, innovation, and influence, among others.", "catalogBreadth": "", "catalogDistr": "", "catalogEnrollmentPriority": null, "catalogLang": "", "catalogForbiddenOverlaps": "", "catalogAttribute": "", "catalogWhenOffered": "Fall.", "catalogComments": "", "catalogPrereqCoreq": "", "catalogPrereq": null, "catalogCoreq": null, "catalogFee": "", "catalogSatisfiesReq": "", "catalogPermission": "", "catalogCourseSubfield": "", "catalogOutcomes": null, "acadCareer": "UG", "acadGroup": "IL", "lastTermsOffered": null, "exploreCriteriaIds": null}, {"strm": 2741, "crseId": 368993, "crseOfferNbr": 1, "subject": "ILROB", "catalogNbr": "4245", "titleShort": "Manage&Lead Soc Innovation", "titleLong": "Managing and Leading Social Innovation", "crseAttrs": [], "crseAttrValueGroups": [], "enrollGroups": [{"classSections": [{"ssrComponent": "LEC", "ssrComponentLong": "Lecture", "section": "001", "classNbr": 14593, "meetings": [{"classMtgNbr": 1, "timeStart": "01:25PM", "timeEnd": "02:40PM", "startDt": "08/29/2019", "endDt": "12/10/2019", "instructors": [{"instrAssignSeq": 1, "netid": "mlb363", "firstName": "Marya", "middleName": "L.", "lastName": "Besharov"}], "pattern": "TR", "facilityDescr": "Ives Hall 111", "bldgDescr": "Ives Hall", "facilityDescrshort": "IVS111", "meetingTopicDescription": ""}], "notes": [], "campus": "MAIN", "campusDescr": "Ithaca", "location": "ITH", "locationDescr": "Ithaca, NY (Main Campus)", "startDt": "08/29/2019", "endDt": "12/10/2019", "addConsent": "N", "addConsentDescr": "No Special Consent Required", "isComponentGraded": true, "crseAttrs": [], "instructionMode": "P", "instrModeDescrshort": "In Person", "instrModeDescr": "In Person", "topicDescription": "", "openStatus": "C", "exploreCriteriaIds": null, "materials": []}], "unitsMinimum": 4, "unitsMaximum": 4, "componentsOptional": [], "componentsRequired": ["LEC"], "gradingBasis": "GRI", "gradingBasisShort": "GradeNoAud", "gradingBasisLong": "Letter grades only (no audit)", "simpleCombinations": [{"subject": "ILROB", "catalogNbr": "6245", "type": "W"}], "sessionCode": "1", "sessionBeginDt": "08/29/2019", "sessionEndDt": "12/10/2019", "sessionLong": "Regular Academic Session", "exploreCriteriaIds": null}], "description": "As the challenges of poverty, income inequality, environmental degradation, and global health continue to mount, there is a growing need for innovative approaches to seemingly intractable social problems. As organizations and their leaders develop new solutions, the boundaries between the for-profit and non-profit sectors have become increasingly blurred. Corporations engage in corporate social responsibility and social intrapreneurship, while traditional non-profits incorporate business practices in an effort to more efficiently and effectively pursue their social missions. In between, there are growing numbers of hybrid social enterprises whose core purpose is to address social issues through commercial ventures. This course explores these varied for-profit, non-profit, and hybrid models for managing and leading social innovation. We will examine the unique challenges and opportunities of each approach and the leadership skills needed to effectively address them. Through readings, class discussion, guest speakers, interactive exercises, and a final project with a community partner, students will master theoretical frameworks and gain practical knowledge and experience that will equip them to manage and lead social innovation in new and existing organizations across a variety of contexts.", "catalogBreadth": "", "catalogDistr": "", "catalogEnrollmentPriority": null, "catalogLang": "", "catalogForbiddenOverlaps": "", "catalogAttribute": "", "catalogWhenOffered": "Fall.", "catalogComments": "", "catalogPrereqCoreq": "", "catalogPrereq": null, "catalogCoreq": null, "catalogFee": "", "catalogSatisfiesReq": "", "catalogPermission": "", "catalogCourseSubfield": "", "catalogOutcomes": null, "acadCareer": "UG", "acadGroup": "IL", "lastTermsOffered": null, "exploreCriteriaIds": null}, {"strm": 2741, "crseId": 355565, "crseOfferNbr": 1, "subject": "ILROB", "catalogNbr": "5200", "titleShort": "Organizational Behavior", "titleLong": "Organizational Behavior", "crseAttrs": [], "crseAttrValueGroups": [], "enrollGroups": [{"classSections": [{"ssrComponent": "LEC", "ssrComponentLong": "Lecture", "section": "001", "classNbr": 14260, "meetings": [{"classMtgNbr": 1, "timeStart": "10:10AM", "timeEnd": "11:25AM", "startDt": "08/29/2019", "endDt": "12/10/2019", "instructors": [{"instrAssignSeq": 1, "netid": "thh2", "firstName": "Tove", "middleName": "Helland", "lastName": "Hammer"}], "pattern": "TR", "facilityDescr": "Ives Hall 215", "bldgDescr": "Ives Hall", "facilityDescrshort": "IVS215", "meetingTopicDescription": ""}], "notes": [], "campus": "MAIN", "campusDescr": "Ithaca", "location": "ITH", "locationDescr": "Ithaca, NY (Main Campus)", "startDt"